--- body ---
## Problem

`scheduler.js` drains each distinct changed item ID by calling `affected(id)`,
inspecting the affected attachments, and then calling `host.unattached()`.
`bootstrap.js` implements `unattached()` as a walk over every Zotero item,
yielding and checking each regular item's `numFileAttachments()`. Thus a burst
of changed IDs repeats a library-wide walk once per ID, even when the event
cannot change whether a bibliographic record has a file. The wall-clock drain
budget from 0796 prevents starvation of admission, but cannot interrupt one
such walk and does not remove the repeated work.

The no-attachment list is separate from attachment coverage. It must still
update after notifications, including when an attachment moves between parents:
the old parent may enter the list and the new parent may leave it in the same
published observation as the attachment's new classification. The existing
reparenting test in `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` pins that behaviour.

## Change

Maintain the no-attachment view by item ID after its initial full census.
For a drained notification, re-evaluate only the regular record named by the
event and any old and new parents whose file-attachment membership may have
changed. Use Zotero's `numFileAttachments()` as the membership rule, so notes
and URL-only attachments do not count as files. Remember the old parent before
inspection changes the parent map; a deleted or moved attachment may no longer
identify it through Zotero. If neither the old map nor the live item identifies
an affected parent, request one full reconciliation rather than silently
claiming the no-attachment view is current. Coalesce parent reads across the
affected IDs in a drain pass.

Publish the affected attachment classifications and no-attachment membership
together. A failure to read a particular record must not invent an absence or
block extraction; retain the last known membership for that record, report a
safe error class, and let the periodic full reconciliation repair the view.
The full-library `unattached()` walk remains at initial and periodic census.
Do not replace it with one full walk per drain pass: with a large backlog, each
budgeted pass could still repeat the same library-wide cost.

This changes the update mechanism, not the definition of attachment coverage,
the no-attachment list, or the scheduler's drain budget. Ticket 0796's
starvation guard remains necessary for other slow or sustained notifications.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js`: dirty-ID drain, `record()`, and atomic
  `censusSnapshot` publication.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js`: `unattached()`, `affected()`, `inspect()`,
  and the parent/child maps.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` and `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`:
  notification bursts, reparenting, and no-attachment membership.
- `verification/incidents/0795-2026-09-15-drain-during-sync.md` and closed
  ticket 0796: the observed bulk-sync backlog and why a drain budget was added.

## Exit criteria

- [x] A failing-before-fix test feeds many changed attachment IDs and counts
      calls to the full-library `unattached()` walk. After the initial census,
      ordinary dirty notifications make **no** full-library walk; an unresolved
      parent may request one coalesced reconciliation. Membership reads scale
      with distinct affected regular records, not with library size times
      dirty-ID count. Instrument actual item/membership reads as well as the
      `unattached()` entry point, so moving the same full walk behind another
      name cannot pass the test.
- [x] Targeted tests cover adding, deleting and moving the only file attachment
      of a regular record; a notification for a record with no file; notes and
      URL-only children; an unreadable or deleted record; and an event whose
      former parent is unknown. The attachment classification and no-attachment
      view agree in each published generation where the needed records are
      readable; the unknown-parent case schedules one reconciliation.
- [x] A later full census repairs a deliberately missed or failed targeted
      update. Rewrite 0796's test feed, which currently injects notifications
      from the soon-to-be-removed per-ID `unattached()` call, so it still proves
      admission under a sustained notification source and no dirty IDs dropped.
- [x] `make check` passes.

## Actions

Read this whole section before touching code; it names the concrete shapes so
the exit criteria can be met without re-deriving the design. Line numbers are
as of the pre-fix tree (2026-09-22) and will drift — treat them as a starting
point, not a guarantee.

### 1. Data structure: an incremental no-attachment Map, keyed by itemID

`bootstrap.js` currently has `unattached()` (~3346) return a fresh array on
every call and keep no state between calls. Give it a module-level `Map`
beside `children`/`parents` (declared at ~3281):

```js
let noAttachment = new Map(); // itemID -> { itemID, libraryID, key, title, parentTitle }
```

`unattached()` keeps doing the full walk, but on success it also rebuilds
`noAttachment` wholesale from the rows it just computed
(`noAttachment = new Map(result.map(row => [row.itemID, row]))`) before
returning `result`. This is the only place the Map is replaced outright; every
other write to it is the incremental patch below. On a read error it leaves
`noAttachment` untouched (same "never invent an absence" rule as the rest of
this ticket) and still returns `result` (currently `[]` on error — unchanged).

Add a new host function, e.g. `refreshUnattached(candidates)`, where
`candidates` is an iterable of itemIDs of *regular* records whose
`numFileAttachments()` membership may have changed. For each id:
- look it up with `Zotero.Items.getAsync`; if it is gone, not regular, or
  deleted, delete it from `noAttachment` (it cannot be a bibliographic record
  with no file if it does not exist as one);
- otherwise call `numFileAttachments()` (after `loadData(['childItems'])`,
  matching `unattached()`'s own sequence at ~3358) and set-or-delete the Map
  entry accordingly, building the row with the same shape `unattached()` uses
  (`title: title || sdtText('file-number', { id })`, `parentTitle: null`);
- wrap the read in try/catch. On a read failure: do **not** touch the Map
  entry for that id (retain last known membership) and call
  `emit('unattached-refresh-error', { error: classifyError(error) }, 'error')`
  — same pattern as the existing `unattached-read-error` emit at ~3371, new
  event name so a test can tell the two apart.

Return `{ list: [...noAttachment.values()], reconcile }` where `reconcile` is
explained in §3. `scheduler.js` assigns `state.unattached = list` directly —
no separate array-diffing needed, the Map already is the source of truth.

### 2. Coalescing mechanism: dedupe candidates within one drain pass

Do **not** call `refreshUnattached` once per retired dirty id (that is the
per-id shape the old `host.unattached()` call had, and reproducing it at a
different name would still cost O(dirty-ID count), just cheaper per call). In
`scheduler.js`'s drain loop (~209-251), the inner
`while (dirty.size && current())` loop is one "drain pass" — bounded by
`SDT_DRAIN_BUDGET_MS`, exited either on an empty `dirty` set or the deadline.

- Before the inner `while`, declare `const candidateParents = new Set();` and
  `let reconcileNeeded = false;` (both local to the pass — a fresh pass starts
  fresh; there is no cross-pass state here, the Map in bootstrap.js is the
  cross-pass state).
- Inside the loop, for each attachment id returned by `host.affected(id)`
  (~232), **before** calling `inspect(affected)`, capture its recorded old
  parent via a new small host accessor (see below), then after `inspect`
  returns, read the new parent off the returned info (see §1's `inspect`
  change below) and add both (when known) to `candidateParents`. The `Set`
  itself is the dedupe: a parent touched by five different dirty ids in the
  same pass is one entry.
- After the inner `while` loop exits (whether by exhaustion or budget), if
  `candidateParents.size || reconcileNeeded`, call
  `const { list, reconcile } = await host.refreshUnattached(candidateParents);`
  once, set `state.unattached = list`, and if `reconcile` (or
  `reconcileNeeded`) set the existing `reconciliationPending = true` (see §3).
  Then `publish()` once more so this generation carries both the last
  retired id's attachment status (already recorded via `record()` inside the
  loop) and the refreshed no-attachment view together, per the Change
  section's "publish together" requirement. This replaces the current
  `if (host.unattached) { state.unattached = await host.unattached(); ... }`
  block (~242-245), which is deleted entirely from the inner loop.
- New host accessor for the pre-inspect old parent, exposed alongside
  `affected`/`inspect` in the `sitter = createSDTSitter({...})` call (~3739):
  `parentOf: id => parents.has(id) ? parents.get(id) : undefined`. This must
  be read **before** `inspect()` runs for that same id in the same iteration
  — `inspect()` (~3469) already captures `previousParent` internally via
  `parents.get(id)` at line 3471, then mutates/deletes the map before
  returning, so a scheduler-side read taken after `inspect()` would see the
  post-mutation state and could no longer name the old parent. This is
  exactly the hazard the Change section's "Remember the old parent before
  inspection changes the parent map" sentence names.
- Extend `inspect()`'s returned `descriptor` (~3505) with one new field,
  `parentItemID: item.parentItemID ?? null`, so the scheduler can read the
  *new* parent off the same call it already makes, instead of adding a second
  host round-trip. The early-return branches (`excluded`/`absent`, no `item`)
  are unaffected — there is no live parent to report there, which is fine:
  the pre-inspect `parentOf(id)` already covered the old-parent side for
  exactly that case.

### 3. Unknown-former-parent fallback: a boolean flag consumed at next `pump()`

`scheduler.js` already has `reconciliationPending` (~110, set `true` by
`start()` and by the hourly `nextReconciliationAt` check at ~150-151) driving
a full census at the top of `pump()`'s outer `while` loop. Reuse it rather
than inventing a second mechanism — it is already coalesced by construction
(a boolean set `true` twice is still just `true`, and it is already checked
once at the top of the very loop the drain pass sits inside, so a flag raised
during a drain pass is consumed by a full census before that same `pump()`
call returns).

Concretely: in the per-id loop of §2, when the pre-inspect `parentOf(affected)`
returns `undefined` (never tracked) **and** the post-inspect `info.absent` is
true (the item is gone and cannot supply a live parent either), set
`reconcileNeeded = true` for this pass instead of adding anything to
`candidateParents` for that id — there is nothing to check. Also thread the
Map-side signal in §1's `refreshUnattached`: if a caller passes it a
sentinel/`undefined` candidate (defensive; should not happen given the above)
it should also report `reconcile: true` rather than throwing. After the pass,
`scheduler.js` sets `reconciliationPending = true` when either source signals
it, which the outer loop's existing `if (reconciliationPending) { ... }`
branch (~154) turns into a full `host.list()` + `host.unattached()` census —
the same path `start()` and the hourly interval already use, so no new
"partial reconciliation" code path is needed.

Do not set `reconciliationPending` unconditionally on every drain pass, and
do not make the coalesced reconciliation a *targeted* re-walk of only the
unknown id — the ticket is explicit that the fallback is "one full
reconciliation", and the existing mechanism already is one.

### 4. Read/error-safety guarantee

Two failure points, both already have a home:
- `refreshUnattached`'s per-candidate try/catch (§1): a record that cannot be
  read keeps its last Map entry (no delete, no phantom add) and emits
  `unattached-refresh-error` with `classifyError(error)` — never let the
  catch block guess membership.
- `inspect()`'s existing `inspection-error` path (bootstrap.js ~3510-3516 and
  scheduler.js's `inspect` wrapper ~104-112) is unchanged; it already returns
  a safe error class and keeps the attachment's last known status via
  `record()`'s "no change on absence of new info" behaviour. Nothing new is
  needed there — cite it in the targeted test (§6) rather than re-implement it.

### 5. First failing test (exit criterion 1)

This needs the *real* `bootstrap.js` + `Zotero` mock
(`tests/sdt_sitter_zotero_mock.mjs`), not the host-level fixture in
`tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` — the scaling claim is about actual
`numFileAttachments()`/`Zotero.Items.getAsync` reads, which only exist behind
the real `unattached()`/`inspect()` implementations bootstrap.js supplies.
The scheduler-level fixture mocks `host.unattached` as an opaque function and
cannot distinguish "walked the whole library" from "checked five records" —
using it here would let a renamed-but-still-O(n) implementation pass.

Add two counters to `tests/sdt_sitter_zotero_mock.mjs`'s `calls` object
(~313-323), since neither exists today and both are load-bearing for "moving
the same full walk behind another name cannot pass":
- `calls.unattachedWalk` (or similar), incremented in `DB.columnQueryAsync`'s
  existing `if (sql.includes('FROM items')) return [...bibliographic.keys()];`
  branch (~622) — that branch is reached *only* by `unattached()`'s own
  `SELECT itemID FROM items ...` query (~3350), so it is a direct, hard-to-fake
  proxy for "a full-library walk ran," independent of what the walk is named.
- `calls.numFileAttachments` (or `membershipReads`), incremented inside
  `regularItem(row).numFileAttachments` (~381-384) every time it is invoked —
  this is the ticket's own chosen membership primitive ("Use Zotero's
  `numFileAttachments()` as the membership rule"), so counting its calls
  measures the real cost regardless of which function calls it.

Test (`tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`), new case:

- Build a harness with several distinct regular records in `unattached`
  (some via `pdf()` attachments giving them a file, some bare so they start
  in the no-attachment view) such that a handful of attachments share a
  small number of distinct parents — e.g. 5 parents, 4 attachments each = 20
  attachments.
- `await h.start()`; record `const baselineWalks = h.calls.unattachedWalk;`
  (should be 1) and `const baselineReads = h.calls.numFileAttachments;`.
- Fire many dirty notifications that only touch those 20 attachments —
  `h.notify('modify', 'item', [id])` repeated, or a loop over all 20 ids, to
  simulate "many changed attachment IDs" — then `await h.quiet();`.
- Assert `h.calls.unattachedWalk === baselineWalks` (no additional
  full-library walk for ordinary dirty notifications).
- Assert `h.calls.numFileAttachments - baselineReads === 5` (or `<= 5`; scales
  with the 5 distinct parents, not the 20 dirty ids or the library size) —
  this is the assertion a same-cost-different-name implementation cannot
  satisfy, since it forces the read count down to the distinct-parent count.
- Assert `h.calls.list === 1` (no extra full census either).

Against the current, unfixed code this fails hard: today's drain loop calls
`host.unattached()` once per *retired dirty id* (20 times, not 1), and each
call re-walks the whole library and re-reads `numFileAttachments()` for every
regular record in the harness, not just the 5 touched ones — both counters
blow past the asserted bounds. That is the red step; write it before any
production-code change.

### 6. Other targeted tests (exit criterion 2) — concrete scenarios

All in `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`, near the existing "unattached
records..." (~2298) and "an attachment moved between parents..." (~3198)
tests; reuse `pdf()`/`h.library.get(id).parentItemID = ...`/`h.notify(...)`
idioms already established there.

- **Moving the only file attachment** — already covered by the existing,
  pinned reparenting test (~3198); do not duplicate, just make sure it still
  passes unmodified (it is one of the exit criteria's explicit anchors).
- **Adding the only file attachment** — start a parent with only a note/link
  child (so it begins in the no-attachment view, as `Parent C` does in the
  reparenting fixture), then reparent an existing file-bearing pdf attachment
  onto it (`h.library.get(pdfId).parentItemID = parentId; h.notify('modify',
  'item', [pdfId]); await h.quiet();`) and assert the parent leaves
  `censusSnapshot.unattached`.
- **Deleting the only file attachment** — start a parent with one pdf
  attachment and no other children; `h.library.get(pdfId).deleted = true;
  h.notify('trash', 'item', [pdfId]);` `await h.quiet();`; assert the parent
  enters `censusSnapshot.unattached`.
- **A notification for a record with no file** — notify directly on a
  bibliographic record id that is already in `unattached` and has no
  children at all (e.g. `h.notify('modify', 'item', [parentIdWithNoChildren])`);
  assert it stays listed and no full census ran (`h.calls.list` unchanged).
- **Notes and URL-only children** — reuse the existing fixture shape (Parent
  C in the reparenting test already has a note + a link-only child and no
  file); add a standalone assertion (or extend that test) that notifying on
  the note or the link directly (`h.notify('modify', 'item', [noteId])`)
  leaves the parent in `unattached` and does not misclassify the note/link
  itself as a file.
- **An unreadable or deleted record** — this needs a new mock capability:
  `tests/sdt_sitter_zotero_mock.mjs` has no existing way to make
  `Zotero.Items.getAsync` throw for a specific id (compare the existing
  `linkModeUnreadable`/`isLinuxThrows` per-scenario throw flags elsewhere in
  the same file). Add a row-level flag, e.g. `unreadable: true`, and make
  `Items.getAsync` throw for that id. Test: notify on an attachment whose
  parent is flagged unreadable; assert the parent's *previous* membership is
  retained in `censusSnapshot.unattached` (not deleted, not invented), and
  that an `unattached-refresh-error` (§4) was emitted with a safe
  `classifyError` value — assert on the emitted event's shape, never on the
  raw error message (this project's `classifyError` discipline, see
  `bootstrap.js` ~641-663).
- **An event whose former parent is unknown** — notify on an itemID that was
  never part of the harness's library at all (so `Items.getAsync` returns
  `null` and `parents.get(id)` was never set): `h.notify('modify', 'item',
  [999999]); await h.quiet();`. Assert `h.calls.list` increased by exactly 1
  over its pre-notify value (the coalesced full reconciliation, §3 — consumed
  within the same `pump()` call per the outer-loop mechanics, so it shows up
  before `quiet()` resolves) and that firing several such unknown ids in one
  drain pass still only adds 1 to `h.calls.list`, not one per id
  (coalescing, §2/§3).

### 7. Rewriting the 0796 sustained-feed test (exit criterion 3)

`tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`'s `'a sustained notification source cannot
starve admission'` test (~1448-1499) currently hooks `f.host.unattached`
(~1478-1483) to do two things on every drain-loop iteration: advance the fake
clock by `STEP_MS` (simulating the per-iteration cost the budget is spent
against) and inject a new dirty id (`f.api.invalidate([1000 + fed++])`) to
keep proving the source outruns the drain. Once `host.unattached()` is no
longer called once per retired id (§2), this hook point disappears and the
test would silently stop exercising sustained pressure at all (it would still
"pass" by doing nothing, which is exactly the false-green shape
`workflow.md`'s diagnosis-discipline section warns about).

Replace the hook with `f.host.affected`, which — unlike `unattached()` — is
still called exactly once per retired dirty id unconditionally, both before
and after this ticket's change (it is not being touched by the redesign;
§1/§2 only change what happens *after* `affected`/`inspect` run). Rewrite:

```js
const affected = f.host.affected;
f.host.affected = async id => {
  now += STEP_MS;
  if (fed < FEED) f.api.invalidate([1000 + fed++]);
  return affected(id);
};
```

Keep every existing assertion (`fed === FEED`, `state.draining === 0`,
`admittedAtFed !== null && admittedAtFed < FEED`, `pendingAtAdmission > 2`) —
they test the drain budget and admission-starvation guarantee, which this
ticket does not change. The rewrite only needs to move *where* the
clock-advance-and-refeed side effect is attached, from a call this ticket
removes to one the redesign does not touch. If `f.host.parentOf` or
`f.host.refreshUnattached` end up added to the scheduler-level fixture too
(only if the scheduler test needs them to avoid throwing — check whether
`createSDTSitter` calls them unconditionally or guards with
`host.parentOf ? ... : ...` the way it already does for `host.unattached ?`),
stub them minimally (e.g. `parentOf: () => undefined, refreshUnattached:
async () => ({ list: [], reconcile: false })`) so the fixture stays a
host-level shape check, not a second copy of bootstrap.js's logic.

### 8. Risk flags

- **YAGNI/premature abstraction**: do not build a generic "reactive
  dependency graph" or a pub/sub layer between `parents`/`children` and the
  no-attachment Map — the ticket's actual shape is one Map, one refresh
  function taking a small candidate set, and one boolean reconciliation flag
  that already exists. Resist generalizing `refreshUnattached` to also drive
  attachment-status recomputation (that is `record()`'s job already and is
  out of scope — the Change section says this ticket "changes the update
  mechanism, not the definition of attachment coverage").
- **Tautological-test risk**: the naive version of exit criterion 1's test —
  asserting only that `host.unattached()`/the walk function is called once —
  would pass a fix that renames the walk or splits it into N per-parent
  calls that still touch the whole library. That is why §5 requires the
  `numFileAttachments()` call-count assertion scaled to distinct parents, not
  just an entry-point call count. Before trusting the test, run it against a
  deliberately wrong "fix" (e.g., one that keeps calling the full
  `unattached()` walk but only once per drain *pass* instead of once per
  drained id) and confirm it still fails — that specific wrong fix is the
  closest plausible near-miss and the one most likely to be implemented by
  accident.
- **Publish-timing risk**: confirm the single post-loop `publish()` in §2
  does not regress the existing "attachment classification and no-attachment
  view agree in each published generation" guarantee the reparenting test
  pins — that test's `await h.quiet()` only reads the final settled state,
  so an implementation that publishes attachment changes eagerly per id and
  the no-attachment refresh once at the end of the pass is still correct as
  long as nothing reads `censusSnapshot` *between* those publishes and acts
  on it as final. Nothing in this codebase currently does, but this is worth
  a one-line note in the eventual PR description if the assumption changes.
